1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:07,528
A thousand years ago, a wooden ship with a dragon on its prow sailed out of the northern mist.

2
00:00:07,748 --> 00:00:12,359
The men aboard did not know it, but they had just found a new world.

3
00:00:12,579 --> 00:00:16,018
Five centuries later, it would be found again...

4
00:00:16,238 --> 00:00:19,618
by a man who never understood what he had found.

5
00:00:28,425 --> 00:00:34,142
The United States is the most powerful political entity that has ever existed.

6
00:00:34,362 --> 00:00:37,940
Call it a superpower. Some would call it an empire.

7
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:43,007
Two thousand years after Rome, we still live inside Rome's echo.

8
00:00:43,227 --> 00:00:47,886
January. February. August, named for the emperor Augustus.

9
00:00:48,106 --> 00:00:52,464
Latin still names every plant and animal known to science.

10
00:00:52,684 --> 00:00:57,560
America's shadow over the next two thousand years may be longer still.

11
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:01,963
And yet it may be the most misunderstood nation on Earth.

12
00:01:03,758 --> 00:01:09,440
So this is a journey through American history, told in order, from the very beginning,

13
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:13,668
through one hundred of the finest books ever written about it.

14
00:01:13,888 --> 00:01:16,636
Not as an expert. As a learner.

15
00:01:16,856 --> 00:01:22,824
It begins with Tony Horwitz's 'A Voyage Long and Strange', a book in three parts.

16
00:01:23,044 --> 00:01:25,825
Discovery. Conquest. Settlement.

17
00:01:26,045 --> 00:01:27,867
Today... Discovery.

18
00:01:33,591 --> 00:01:36,833
The year is 793.

19
00:01:37,053 --> 00:01:42,523
Longships beach on Lindisfarne, a holy island off the coast of England.

20
00:01:42,743 --> 00:01:46,724
The raiders sack its monastery and carry off its gold.

21
00:01:46,944 --> 00:01:49,403
The Viking Age has begun.

22
00:01:51,216 --> 00:01:56,111
Who were they? Norse people, from what is now Norway, Denmark and Sweden.

23
00:01:56,331 --> 00:01:58,993
Most of them were simply farmers.

24
00:01:59,213 --> 00:02:05,196
Only those who went raiding, sailing out to plunder and conquer, were truly called Vikings.

25
00:02:05,416 --> 00:02:08,346
Today we give that name to all of them.

26
00:02:10,133 --> 00:02:15,293
It was an age of legends. Ragnar Lothbrok, half myth, half man.

27
00:02:15,513 --> 00:02:21,106
Rollo, the Viking who took a piece of France: Normandy, the land of the Northmen.

28
00:02:21,326 --> 00:02:27,528
His descendant, William the Conqueror, would seize England in 1066.

29
00:02:27,748 --> 00:02:31,100
Every English monarch since descends from him.

30
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:37,998
That same year, the last great Viking king, Harald Hardrada, died trying to conquer England.

31
00:02:38,218 --> 00:02:41,666
And with him, the Viking Age came to an end.

32
00:02:43,466 --> 00:02:48,615
But before it ended, the Norse pushed west, across the grey North Atlantic.

33
00:02:48,835 --> 00:02:52,710
To Iceland: a raw land of volcanoes and geysers,

34
00:02:52,930 --> 00:02:56,481
whose ash made the soil surprisingly fertile.

35
00:02:56,701 --> 00:02:58,351
There, they settled.

36
00:03:08,550 --> 00:03:12,504
Among them was a man named Eiríkr. Erik the Red.

37
00:03:12,724 --> 00:03:14,563
A hothead. A killer.

38
00:03:14,783 --> 00:03:19,975
Banished from his homeland for murder, he settled in Iceland... and killed again.

39
00:03:20,195 --> 00:03:23,820
Banished once more, he did the only thing left to do.

40
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:25,612
He sailed west.

41
00:03:27,425 --> 00:03:32,212
Around the year 985, he found a vast island.

42
00:03:32,432 --> 00:03:36,999
The climate was warmer then. But even so, he was selling a dream.

43
00:03:37,219 --> 00:03:38,963
He called it Greenland.

44
00:03:40,758 --> 00:03:45,557
Today, around eighty percent of it lies beneath a thick sheet of ice.

45
00:03:45,777 --> 00:03:50,185
Erik may have been history's first great real-estate salesman.

46
00:03:50,405 --> 00:03:55,229
It worked. Settlers followed, and Erik ruled them as their overlord.

47
00:03:57,050 --> 00:04:04,600
Then a sailor named Bjarni Herjólfsson, bound for Greenland, was caught in a storm and blown far off course.

48
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:08,702
Out of the fog rose a strange, forested coast.

49
00:04:08,922 --> 00:04:12,540
His crew begged to go ashore. Bjarni refused.

50
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:16,344
Nothing worth seeing here, he said, and turned back.

51
00:04:16,564 --> 00:04:22,717
For a thousand years, he has been remembered as a man of spectacularly poor judgment.

52
00:04:22,937 --> 00:04:25,408
He had just seen North America.

53
00:04:31,133 --> 00:04:34,479
Erik's son Leif was nothing like his father.

54
00:04:34,699 --> 00:04:36,803
Steady. Calm. Careful.

55
00:04:37,023 --> 00:04:39,949
He bought Bjarni's ship, gathered a crew,

56
00:04:40,169 --> 00:04:44,013
and went looking for the land that Bjarni had refused.

57
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:52,642
First came a coast of bare, flat rock. Helluland: the Land of Stone Slabs. Baffin Island.

58
00:04:52,862 --> 00:04:58,339
Then a coast of endless forest. Markland, the Forest Land. Labrador.

59
00:04:58,559 --> 00:05:04,161
And then, further south, a place where they finally came ashore, and built houses.

60
00:05:04,381 --> 00:05:09,201
Around the year one thousand, Europeans were living in North America.

61
00:05:11,008 --> 00:05:14,740
One of Leif's men wandered off, and came back... drunk.

62
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:19,236
In the far north, where grapes do not grow, this was a mystery.

63
00:05:19,456 --> 00:05:23,706
He had found grapes, he said. Wild vines, heavy with fruit.

64
00:05:23,926 --> 00:05:28,296
And so the land had its name. Vinland. The Land of Wine.

65
00:05:28,516 --> 00:05:31,920
Leif sailed home a legend. Leif the Lucky.

66
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:33,757
He never went back.

67
00:05:39,466 --> 00:05:44,222
Leif's brother Thorvald followed in his footsteps, and found the houses.

68
00:05:44,442 --> 00:05:47,659
One day, his men saw three humps on the beach.

69
00:05:47,879 --> 00:05:51,742
Not rocks. Canoes, with men sheltering beneath them.

70
00:05:51,962 --> 00:05:54,694
The Norse attacked. Eight were killed.

71
00:05:54,914 --> 00:05:56,415
One escaped.

72
00:05:58,216 --> 00:06:00,351
He came back with a fleet.

73
00:06:00,571 --> 00:06:02,654
Arrows darkened the sky.

74
00:06:02,874 --> 00:06:06,513
Thorvald was struck, and knew the wound was fatal.

75
00:06:06,733 --> 00:06:10,708
He asked to be buried in the land he had hoped to call home.

76
00:06:10,928 --> 00:06:15,491
He was among the first Europeans known to die in the Americas.

77
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:22,149
The Norse called the natives Skraelings. Roughly: wretches. Screechers.

78
00:06:22,369 --> 00:06:24,622
A word like 'barbarian'.

79
00:06:24,842 --> 00:06:31,620
Of course, to the natives, these pale, red-bearded strangers must have looked every bit as strange.

80
00:06:37,341 --> 00:06:43,421
Another brother, Thorstein, set out to bring Thorvald's body home, but fell sick and died.

81
00:06:43,641 --> 00:06:48,610
His widow, Gudrid, remarried a merchant captain, Thorfinn Karlsefni.

82
00:06:48,830 --> 00:06:53,049
Together they sailed to Vinland, with livestock and settlers.

83
00:06:53,269 --> 00:06:55,258
This time, there was trade.

84
00:06:57,050 --> 00:07:02,615
The natives, who had never seen dairy, traded furs and goods for milk and cheese.

85
00:07:02,835 --> 00:07:06,924
And in Vinland, Gudrid gave birth to a son. Snorri.

86
00:07:07,144 --> 00:07:12,338
The first child of European descent known to be born in the Americas.

87
00:07:14,133 --> 00:07:18,394
Then came Erik's daughter, Freydis. As fierce as her father.

88
00:07:18,614 --> 00:07:22,366
She sailed with two brothers who captained a second ship.

89
00:07:22,586 --> 00:07:28,822
They agreed on equal crews. She secretly smuggled five extra warriors aboard her own.

90
00:07:29,042 --> 00:07:30,574
Numbers matter.

91
00:07:30,794 --> 00:07:34,745
In Vinland, she drove the brothers out of Leif's houses.

92
00:07:36,550 --> 00:07:40,371
Then one night she came back to bed, cold and barefoot,

93
00:07:40,591 --> 00:07:45,065
and told her husband that the brothers had struck and insulted her.

94
00:07:45,285 --> 00:07:48,532
If you were a man, she said, you would avenge me.

95
00:07:50,341 --> 00:07:52,773
Her men killed the brothers' crew.

96
00:07:52,993 --> 00:07:58,761
When they refused to kill the five women, Freydis took up an axe and did it herself.

97
00:07:58,981 --> 00:08:03,186
Later, heavily pregnant, she faced down a native attack.

98
00:08:03,406 --> 00:08:08,250
But the Norse were few. The natives were many, and could always bring more.

99
00:08:08,470 --> 00:08:11,446
The Norse sailed away, and never came back.

100
00:08:17,175 --> 00:08:19,071
Then the world forgot.

101
00:08:19,291 --> 00:08:25,468
For nearly five hundred years, no one in Europe remembered that there was land to the west.

102
00:08:25,688 --> 00:08:35,991
Not until the 1960s did archaeologists find the Norse settlement at L'Anse aux Meadows, on the northern tip of Newfoundland.

103
00:08:36,211 --> 00:08:39,428
Low sod houses, dark as hobbit holes.

104
00:08:39,648 --> 00:08:44,071
It became one of the very first UNESCO World Heritage Sites.

105
00:08:45,883 --> 00:08:48,544
And the people the Norse had met?

106
00:08:48,764 --> 00:08:54,568
When Europeans returned, English, French, Portuguese and Basque, to fish and to hunt,

107
00:08:54,788 --> 00:08:59,329
they came into conflict with the island's natives: the Beothuk.

108
00:08:59,549 --> 00:09:02,663
They were hunted, pushed from the coast, starved.

109
00:09:02,883 --> 00:09:09,490
In 1823, three Beothuk women were captured. Two soon died.

110
00:09:09,710 --> 00:09:16,289
The last, Shanawdithit, died of tuberculosis in 1829.

111
00:09:16,509 --> 00:09:19,851
After her, no Beothuk was ever seen again.

112
00:09:21,633 --> 00:09:24,809
Today, Newfoundland is part of Canada.

113
00:09:25,029 --> 00:09:30,161
And five centuries after Leif, the second discovery was about to begin,

114
00:09:30,381 --> 00:09:32,837
by a man who was always lost.

115
00:09:38,550 --> 00:09:41,929
Cristoforo Colombo. Christopher Columbus.

116
00:09:42,149 --> 00:09:48,474
Born in Genoa around 1451, the son of a weaver.

117
00:09:48,694 --> 00:09:53,506
He went to sea, sailed the coast of West Africa and its slave ports,

118
00:09:53,726 --> 00:09:57,251
and made his life in Portugal and in Spain.

119
00:09:59,050 --> 00:10:03,239
A popular myth says that Europe believed the world was flat.

120
00:10:03,459 --> 00:10:07,922
It did not. Educated Europeans knew the Earth was a sphere.

121
00:10:08,142 --> 00:10:10,829
The only question was: how big?

122
00:10:12,633 --> 00:10:18,761
In 1453, the Ottoman Turks conquered Constantinople.

123
00:10:18,981 --> 00:10:27,386
The old roads to the riches of the East, to India's spices, to the China of Marco Polo and the Great Khan, were cut.

124
00:10:27,606 --> 00:10:31,867
Whoever found a new way there would become impossibly rich.

125
00:10:33,675 --> 00:10:37,888
The Portuguese went south, feeling their way around Africa.

126
00:10:38,108 --> 00:10:44,488
Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488.

127
00:10:44,708 --> 00:10:48,764
A decade later, Vasco da Gama would reach India.

128
00:10:48,984 --> 00:10:52,111
Columbus had a different idea. Go west.

129
00:10:53,925 --> 00:10:59,518
He believed Asia lay only about five thousand kilometers west of Spain.

130
00:10:59,738 --> 00:11:03,390
The real distance is closer to eighteen thousand.

131
00:11:03,610 --> 00:11:06,736
He was wrong by an ocean, and a continent.

132
00:11:08,550 --> 00:11:11,143
Portugal laughed him out of court.

133
00:11:11,363 --> 00:11:18,477
But Spain, newly united under Ferdinand and Isabella, and hungry to outdo its rivals, listened.

134
00:11:18,697 --> 00:11:22,744
Columbus promised them the thing every kingdom craved.

135
00:11:22,964 --> 00:11:24,003
Gold.

136
00:11:29,716 --> 00:11:33,837
Three ships: the Niña, the Pinta, and the Santa María.

137
00:11:34,057 --> 00:11:39,754
After resupplying in the Canary Islands, they sailed west, into the unknown.

138
00:11:41,550 --> 00:11:45,259
As days became weeks, his sailors grew afraid.

139
00:11:45,479 --> 00:11:50,800
Columbus kept two logs: the true distance, and a shorter one he showed his crew.

140
00:11:51,020 --> 00:11:55,061
No one had ever sailed so long into the open ocean.

141
00:11:56,841 --> 00:12:04,672
The crown promised a lifetime pension of ten thousand maravedís a year to the first man to sight land.

142
00:12:04,892 --> 00:12:13,678
On the twelfth of October, 1492, a lookout on the Pinta, Rodrigo de Triana, cried out.

143
00:12:13,898 --> 00:12:14,905
Land!

144
00:12:15,125 --> 00:12:20,533
But Columbus claimed he had seen a light the night before, and took the reward for himself.

145
00:12:22,341 --> 00:12:26,761
Somewhere in the Bahamas, they waded ashore and kissed the sand.

146
00:12:26,981 --> 00:12:32,327
The people who greeted them, the Taíno, were friendly, curious and generous.

147
00:12:32,547 --> 00:12:37,038
They traded parrots and cotton for glass beads and little bells.

148
00:12:37,258 --> 00:12:43,855
The Spaniards were astonished. The islanders were taller, healthier, and far cleaner than the Europeans.

149
00:12:44,075 --> 00:12:45,947
But they had no metal.

150
00:12:46,167 --> 00:12:51,056
When they were shown swords, they grasped them by the blade, and cut themselves.

151
00:12:52,841 --> 00:12:55,745
That night, Columbus wrote in his journal:

152
00:12:55,965 --> 00:12:57,813
They should be good servants.

153
00:12:59,633 --> 00:13:04,963
Tiny flakes of gold glinted in their ornaments. Gold meant a source, somewhere.

154
00:13:05,183 --> 00:13:09,769
Columbus pushed on to Cuba, sure that he was near the Ganges,

155
00:13:09,989 --> 00:13:15,519
then east, to a great island he named La Isla Española: Hispaniola.

156
00:13:15,739 --> 00:13:19,498
Today it holds Haiti and the Dominican Republic.

157
00:13:21,299 --> 00:13:28,698
On Christmas Eve, 1492, the Santa María ran aground and was lost.

158
00:13:28,918 --> 00:13:34,114
From her timbers, Columbus built a fort. La Navidad, the Nativity.

159
00:13:34,334 --> 00:13:41,140
He left thirty-nine men behind with orders: find the gold, and do not make enemies of the natives.

160
00:13:41,360 --> 00:13:43,145
Then he sailed home.

161
00:13:44,924 --> 00:13:50,834
In Spain, Columbus announced that he had reached the Indies, where rivers ran with gold.

162
00:13:51,054 --> 00:13:56,234
He was named Admiral of the Ocean Sea, and governor of every land he found,

163
00:13:56,454 --> 00:13:58,956
with a share of all their riches.

164
00:14:04,674 --> 00:14:11,332
In 1493 he returned. Not with three ships, but seventeen.

165
00:14:11,552 --> 00:14:16,126
Twelve hundred men, many of them gentlemen hungry for fortune.

166
00:14:16,346 --> 00:14:22,325
Island-hopping, he named as he went. Dominica. Guadeloupe. The Virgin Islands.

167
00:14:22,545 --> 00:14:26,664
Whatever those islands were called before, no one remembers.

168
00:14:28,466 --> 00:14:31,756
At La Navidad, he found only corpses.

169
00:14:31,976 --> 00:14:35,689
Through captives who now spoke Spanish, he learned why.

170
00:14:35,909 --> 00:14:40,738
His men had seized native women, and the islanders had killed them all.

171
00:14:42,549 --> 00:14:48,000
Columbus was no longer an explorer. He was a governor. And a disastrous one.

172
00:14:48,220 --> 00:14:50,201
He enslaved the island.

173
00:14:50,421 --> 00:14:55,671
Every native over fourteen owed a quota of gold, every three months.

174
00:14:55,891 --> 00:15:01,013
Those who failed were brutally punished. Many were mutilated, or killed.

175
00:15:02,799 --> 00:15:09,142
Men were sent to the mines. Women, to the cassava fields. Apart, for months at a time.

176
00:15:09,362 --> 00:15:12,269
Children went uncared for, and died.

177
00:15:12,489 --> 00:15:17,237
Exhausted and in despair, people stopped having children at all.

178
00:15:17,457 --> 00:15:21,691
Within a decade, perhaps a third of the island's people were dead.

179
00:15:23,508 --> 00:15:25,758
Still, there was no great gold.

180
00:15:25,978 --> 00:15:33,376
So in 1496, Columbus sailed home with enslaved Taíno instead.

181
00:15:33,596 --> 00:15:39,791
Many died on the crossing, and were thrown overboard. Most of the rest died in Spain.

182
00:15:40,011 --> 00:15:46,638
His reputation began to crack. And the crown ended his monopoly on voyages west.

183
00:15:52,341 --> 00:15:59,875
In 1498, on his third voyage, he steered by the stars, and got lost again.

184
00:16:00,095 --> 00:16:04,039
He reached the coast of Venezuela. South America.

185
00:16:04,259 --> 00:16:08,419
Faced with a vast river pouring fresh water into the sea,

186
00:16:08,639 --> 00:16:14,367
he wondered whether the Earth was shaped like a pear, with the Garden of Eden at its tip.

187
00:16:16,174 --> 00:16:22,008
On Hispaniola, the colony was in chaos. Infighting. Mutiny. Rebellion.

188
00:16:22,228 --> 00:16:26,667
In 1500, the crown sent an investigator.

189
00:16:26,887 --> 00:16:33,161
He arrested the Admiral of the Ocean Sea, put him in chains, and shipped him back to Spain.

190
00:16:34,966 --> 00:16:41,005
Columbus talked his way into one last voyage, in 1502.

191
00:16:41,225 --> 00:16:46,655
His worm-eaten ships fell apart, and he was marooned on Jamaica for a year.

192
00:16:46,875 --> 00:16:52,682
He returned to Spain in 1504, arthritic and broken.

193
00:16:52,902 --> 00:16:59,087
In 1506, he died, still insisting he had reached the Indies.

194
00:17:04,799 --> 00:17:15,047
Meanwhile, a Florentine named Amerigo Vespucci claimed to have reached the new lands in 1497. Probably a lie.

195
00:17:15,267 --> 00:17:20,405
He told of women so tall that, kneeling, they towered over standing men.

196
00:17:20,625 --> 00:17:23,548
His stories sold. Europe was hooked.

197
00:17:25,341 --> 00:17:34,896
In 1507, a German mapmaker, Martin Waldseemüller, understood that this was not India, but a new world,

198
00:17:35,116 --> 00:17:37,553
and named it after Vespucci.

199
00:17:37,773 --> 00:17:38,966
America.

200
00:17:39,186 --> 00:17:45,798
He later changed his mind. But the great cartographer Mercator used the name again, and it stuck.

201
00:17:46,018 --> 00:17:50,616
Columbus got the District of Columbia, and a country, Colombia.

202
00:17:50,836 --> 00:17:53,317
A salesman got two continents.

203
00:17:59,049 --> 00:18:06,540
Even in death, Columbus kept wandering. He was buried in Valladolid in 1506.

204
00:18:06,760 --> 00:18:10,876
Moved to Seville in 1509.

205
00:18:11,096 --> 00:18:22,271
In 1541, shipped across the Atlantic to the cathedral of Santo Domingo, and buried in an unmarked spot, for fear of pirates.

206
00:18:22,491 --> 00:18:31,193
In 1795, as Spain surrendered Hispaniola to France, his bones were moved to Havana.

207
00:18:31,413 --> 00:18:39,058
Mark Twain later joked that Havana displayed two skulls of Columbus: one as a boy, and one as a man.

208
00:18:39,278 --> 00:18:46,006
And when Spain lost Cuba in 1898, the bones went back to Seville.

209
00:18:47,799 --> 00:18:55,864
But in 1877, workers in Santo Domingo's cathedral found a lead box.

210
00:18:56,084 --> 00:18:59,009
Inside: bones, and an inscription.

211
00:18:59,229 --> 00:19:03,647
Illustrious and distinguished man, Don Cristóbal Colón.

212
00:19:03,867 --> 00:19:07,656
So whose bones went to Seville? His son Diego's?

213
00:19:07,876 --> 00:19:11,912
Spain and the Dominican Republic both still claim him.

214
00:19:13,716 --> 00:19:19,909
In Santo Domingo stands his tomb: a vast monument called the Columbus Lighthouse.

215
00:19:20,129 --> 00:19:25,180
Its searchlights can project a cross into the clouds, visible for miles.

216
00:19:25,400 --> 00:19:30,063
But on an island of frequent blackouts, the lights mostly stay dark.

217
00:19:30,283 --> 00:19:34,229
Dominicans speak of the fukú. The curse of Columbus.

218
00:19:34,449 --> 00:19:38,353
Put his name on something, they say, and watch it fail.

219
00:19:44,050 --> 00:19:53,193
In 1502, an eighteen-year-old Spaniard named Bartolomé de las Casas arrived on Hispaniola.

220
00:19:53,413 --> 00:19:56,114
He became a colonist, and a priest.

221
00:19:56,334 --> 00:20:00,154
The system around him was called the encomienda.

222
00:20:00,374 --> 00:20:08,573
With a stroke of the pen, the land belonged to the crown, and the Taíno were parcelled out to Spanish masters to work it.

223
00:20:08,793 --> 00:20:11,000
Slavery, in all but name.

224
00:20:12,800 --> 00:20:20,556
In 1511, a friar named Antonio de Montesinos thundered from the pulpit.

225
00:20:20,776 --> 00:20:25,021
Are these not men? Are you not obliged to love them as yourselves?

226
00:20:25,241 --> 00:20:28,053
Las Casas had a change of heart.

227
00:20:28,273 --> 00:20:33,135
He gave up his own natives, and spent the rest of his life bearing witness.

228
00:20:34,925 --> 00:20:41,620
His book, A Short Account of the Destruction of the Indies, records what he saw with his own eyes.

229
00:20:41,840 --> 00:20:45,517
Spaniards who forced natives to carry them everywhere.

230
00:20:45,737 --> 00:20:51,241
Men who wagered on whether they could cut a person in two with a single stroke.

231
00:20:51,461 --> 00:20:54,284
People burned over slow fires.

232
00:20:56,091 --> 00:21:04,322
In 1492, Hispaniola was home to at least half a million people. Perhaps several million.

233
00:21:04,542 --> 00:21:13,130
In 1514, a Spanish census counted fewer than twenty-three thousand working-age natives.

234
00:21:13,350 --> 00:21:18,816
Within a few more decades, the Taíno of Hispaniola were effectively gone.

235
00:21:19,036 --> 00:21:21,292
There is only one word for it.

236
00:21:23,091 --> 00:21:30,893
Las Casas, desperate to spare the Taíno, urged that enslaved Africans be brought to do the work instead,

237
00:21:31,113 --> 00:21:34,383
a position he would later bitterly regret.

238
00:21:34,603 --> 00:21:38,118
It became standard practice across the Americas.

239
00:21:38,338 --> 00:21:43,187
Wherever the native population collapsed, ships arrived from Africa.

240
00:21:44,966 --> 00:21:48,189
And yet, the Taíno live on, in words.

241
00:21:48,409 --> 00:21:52,666
Hamaca: hammock. Canoa: canoe. Huracán: hurricane.

242
00:21:52,886 --> 00:21:58,455
Barbacoa: barbecue. Sabana: savanna. Tabaco. And batata: potato.

243
00:21:58,675 --> 00:22:04,981
And the crops of the Americas, maize, cassava, potatoes, peppers, peanuts, pineapples,

244
00:22:05,201 --> 00:22:07,616
now feed the whole world.

245
00:22:13,341 --> 00:22:16,800
Why did the Vikings fail, where Spain succeeded?

246
00:22:17,020 --> 00:22:20,650
The Norse had steel too. But the Spaniards had more.

247
00:22:20,870 --> 00:22:29,201
Horses, which no one in the Americas had ever seen. Some believed that horse and rider were a single, monstrous creature.

248
00:22:29,421 --> 00:22:33,878
Firearms. Crude and inaccurate, but terrifying in their thunder.

249
00:22:34,098 --> 00:22:36,830
And above all, money and numbers.

250
00:22:37,050 --> 00:22:42,785
The Vinland voyages were small family ventures: one family, four voyages.

251
00:22:43,005 --> 00:22:48,398
Spain had the treasury of a kingdom behind it, and ship after ship after ship.

252
00:22:50,216 --> 00:22:56,565
Spain would go on to colonize most of South and Central America, and much of North America.

253
00:22:56,785 --> 00:23:01,761
Today, more Spanish speakers live in the United States than in Spain.

254
00:23:03,550 --> 00:23:09,725
Columbus never saw the land that became the United States. Neither did the Vikings.

255
00:23:09,945 --> 00:23:11,872
But they opened the door.

256
00:23:12,092 --> 00:23:21,846
The English would come later, Jamestown in 1607, the Pilgrims at Plymouth in 1620,

257
00:23:22,066 --> 00:23:24,839
and they would build something very different.

258
00:23:26,633 --> 00:23:29,756
History is not here to pass judgment.

259
00:23:29,976 --> 00:23:35,309
It is a story of wars, of conquest, of civilizations that vanished.

260
00:23:35,529 --> 00:23:39,073
The point is to understand cause and effect.

261
00:23:39,293 --> 00:23:42,575
How the modern world came to be the way it is.

262
00:23:44,383 --> 00:23:46,176
This was Discovery.

263
00:23:46,396 --> 00:23:49,387
Next time: Conquest... and Settlement.

